051059 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186213042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051059 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186213074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051059 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186213075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051059 #edb/ref 1031 "Rewriting Radical Expressions Using the Laws of Exponents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051059 #edb/ref 1033 6206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051059 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7dc8672e-e7ec-5a52-965b-6b7d5fb00896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067924 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067924 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067924 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067924 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e0f3f146-a1db-5130-a033-9f2145c46273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067929 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{k}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067929 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067929 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7a167714-0fab-51e5-889f-1955dd7dde48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068437 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068437 #edb/ref 1071 "Write $(2a)^{1/3}$ using radical notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d956f2f-da17-5f0a-9eee-d1a8d1aeab0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068437 #edb/ref 1074 "In general, the fractional exponent $x^{1/n}$ can also be written as the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}.$ In this particular case, $n=3,$ $x=2a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n(2a)^{1/3} = \\sqrt[3]{2a}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068438 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068438 #edb/ref 1071 "Write $\\sqrt[4]{2y}$ using power notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96c1a35f-ee50-5546-bc26-cff2a4e43df8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068438 #edb/ref 1074 "In general, the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ can also be written using a fractional exponent as $x^{1/n}.$ In this particular case, $n=4,$ $x=2y,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{2y} = (2y)^{1/4}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068440 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068440 #edb/ref 1071 "Write $x^{-1/2}$ using radical notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068440 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b4f5483-d272-5605-a7cc-9c9f11653707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068440 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that, in general, a negative exponent can be converted to a positive exponent if we take the reciprocal. Here, we have\n\n$$\nx^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{x^{1/2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual to convert the fractional exponent to a radical\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^{1/2}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx^{-1/2} =  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068441 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1067" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068441 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{|}]{2y}}$ using power notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068441 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6cb6f11f-cdf8-5082-8ccf-2052d15fb876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068441 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{|}]{2y}} = \\dfrac{1}{(2y)^{1/3}}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we take the reciprocal and convert the exponent to a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(2y)^{1/3}} = (2y)^{-1/3}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{|}]{2y}} = (2y)^{-1/3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068818 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068818 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068818 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting a Fractional Exponent to a Radical" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068818 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068818 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8f568f1f-5c73-5080-893c-f0bde7e44270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068819 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting a Radical to a Fractional Exponent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068819 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068819 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b87f622b-5a30-5cb8-88f5-6b91450deb07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068820 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068820 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting a Negative Fractional Exponent to a Radical" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068820 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068440 #edb/ref 17592186