186069459 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "874e0e5d-1955-54ec-9b68-2ef62d277db8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069459 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that in general, a negative exponent can be converted to a positive exponent if we take the reciprocal. Here, we have:\n\n$$\n(5k)^{-1/3}=\\frac{1}{(5k)^{1/3}}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual to convert the fractional exponent to a radical:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(5k)^{1/3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{5k}}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n(5k)^{-1/3}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{5k}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069751 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069751 #edb/ref 1061 3500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069751 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e166ad5-7eaf-5c09-b0e8-453bb6131e54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069751 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069752 #edb/ref 1061 3503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3032e04b-8a8c-560a-a76a-876ce6998f52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069753 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069753 #edb/ref 1061 3504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069753 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9934437-0ac8-5a9d-8fcf-4cdf587db101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069753 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069754 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069754 #edb/ref 1061 3505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069754 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4602e249-8912-5228-a874-e070850a988f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069754 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069755 #edb/ref 1061 3506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069755 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "953bf11e-3461-5bd8-8422-9c7bd8e113dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069755 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069937 #edb/ref 1064 "A radical expression like $\\sqrt{{\\color{black}x}}$ can also be written using a fractional exponent as $x^{1/2}.$ They mean the same thing.\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- a cube root such as $\\sqrt[{\\color{blue}3\\!\\phantom{p}}]{x}$ is equivalent to $x^{1/\\color{blue}{3}},$\n\n- a tenth root $\\sqrt[{\\color{blue}10\\!\\phantom{p}}]{x}$ is equivalent to $x^{1/\\color{blue}{10}},$\n\nand so on. In general, we have the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069937 #edb/ref 1065 1475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069937 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a261c9ab-28f0-59c3-ae99-985dc03258d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069937 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483577 #edb/ref 1080 "{t}^{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483577 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483577 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "053123e5-3a18-570e-ae84-136e92cd32a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483579 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt[7]{4y}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483579 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483579 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06832b2f-02b8-5c41-951c-ac166fee297f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483585 #edb/ref 1080 "(9t)^{-3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483585 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483585 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07d9a016-b134-5db2-9710-01edb7ff247e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483593 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{4y}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483593 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483593 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1031089c-5449-50b9-8e55-6d01405d29d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483597 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{9v}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483597 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483597 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11e9b5d